{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "$\\beta$ **Decay**\nIn the type of $\\beta$ decay discussed here, a neutron in the nucleus becomes a proton and an electron is produced. The electron was not stored inside the nucleus beforehand."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The proton number, and therefore the nuclear charge, increases by $1$;\nthe mass number stays the same and the neutron number decreases by $1$."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "In this $\\beta$ process, the proton remains in the nucleus and the electron leaves it. The complete process also includes the antineutrino introduced below.\nA neutron can remain stable in a suitable nucleus;\nthis does not mean that every bound neutron is stable."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The same kind of initial nucleus can undergo the same decay and emit electrons with different energies while producing the same final nucleus.\nIf only the final nucleus and the electron are included in the energy account, some energy appears to be missing.\nThis once raised doubts about energy conservation. It did not prove that energy was destroyed: a decay product was missing from the account."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Pauli's hypothesis**\nPauli, a physicist working in Switzerland, proposed an additional particle that the instruments of the time had not detected."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "It was produced together with the proton and electron and carried away the missing energy.\nThe original textbook described it using an electrically neutral, massless approximation. Its very weak interaction with matter makes detection difficult;\nit is not absolutely incapable of causing ionization or a nuclear reaction."
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "A particle that never interacted with anything would make this explanation impossible to test: energy would seem to disappear with it forever.\nPauli's proposal was different. The interaction was extremely weak, not absent.\nThe particle could therefore pass through thick material while still having a chance of being detected."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Fermi named the particle the **neutrino**, meaning \"little neutron.\"\nThe original textbook wrote its rest mass as $0$ and, within that massless approximation, said it could not be at rest and moved at $300000$ kilometers per second as soon as it was produced."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "These are historical approximations in the textbook, not precise present-day conclusions about neutrino mass and speed.\nCalculations showed that interactions in a given thickness of material would be very rare, making detection difficult.\nThe comparison \"Earth is more transparent to neutrinos than glass is to light\" emphasizes their strong penetrating ability.\nLater neutrino-oscillation experiments disproved the old assumption that neutrinos are massless; their absolute masses remain under study."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Free-neutron decay**\nNeutrinos occur not only in nuclear $\\beta$ decay but also in the spontaneous decay of some free particles, including the neutron."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Nuclear interactions can keep a neutron stable in a suitable nucleus, whereas a free neutron decays.\nThe textbook gave its mean lifetime approximately as 16 minutes;"
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "this is not a precise modern measurement.\nExperiments need neutron sources, and nuclear reactors can provide intense neutron beams."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "This lesson distinguishes the neutrino (symbol $\\nu$) from the antineutrino (symbol $\\bar{\\nu}$) in this process.\nWhen a neutron becomes a proton and emits an electron, an antineutrino is emitted."
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "$n \\rightarrow p + e^{-} + \\bar{\\nu}$"
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The energy account for this decay is incomplete if it includes only the proton and electron.\nThe energy carried away by the antineutrino must also be included to apply energy conservation."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Experimental discovery**\nAbout 26 years passed between the hypothesis and the experimental discovery."
    },
    {
      "index": 16,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The instruments recorded reaction products, not a directly visible neutrino track.\nTheory predicted that an antineutrino with sufficient energy could interact with a proton and produce a neutron and a positron:"
    },
    {
      "index": 17,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "$p + \\bar{\\nu} \\rightarrow n + e^{+}$"
    },
    {
      "index": 18,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "An antineutrino rarely interacts with matter, but a sufficiently large number can produce detectable events.\nUranium fission in a reactor creates many short-lived $\\beta$-radioactive fragments, producing many antineutrinos."
    },
    {
      "index": 19,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The experiment described in the textbook took place in 1956 in the United States: a detector near the reactor was placed underground and shielded with lead and paraffin.\nThe textbook describes 200 liters of water and about 300 liters of liquid scintillator around it.\nA $\\gamma$ quantum passing through the scintillator produces a flash. This is the original book's outline of the apparatus, not instructions for performing the experiment."
    },
    {
      "index": 20,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "An antineutrino interaction with a proton in the water produces a positron (point $A$ in Figure 277).\nThe positron subsequently annihilates with an electron at $B$, producing 2 $\\gamma$ quanta."
    },
    {
      "index": 21,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "These $\\gamma$ quanta make the scintillator flash, providing the prompt signal.\nThe neutron produced in the reaction undergoes scattering before a cadmium nucleus added to the water captures it at $C$."
    },
    {
      "index": 22,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The excited nucleus then emits several $\\gamma$ quanta, providing a delayed signal.\nThe two early $\\gamma$ quanta traveling in different directions and the later $\\gamma$ quanta help identify antineutrino events."
    },
    {
      "index": 23,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "Reliable identification also requires rejecting background events;\nany pair of flashes is not automatically proof of an antineutrino interaction."
    },
    {
      "index": 24,
      "blockIndex": 15,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**What particle decay means**\nParticle decay is a transformation. It must not always be imagined as a complicated object splitting into parts that were already stored inside it.\nThis statement alone does not establish whether a particular particle has smaller constituents."
    },
    {
      "index": 25,
      "blockIndex": 16,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Decay products are not related to the initial particle in the same way that broken pieces are related to an intact clay pot.**\nThe textbook used the claim that an antineutrino moves at the speed of light to argue that it was not stored inside the neutron beforehand."
    },
    {
      "index": 26,
      "blockIndex": 16,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "That speed claim belongs to the historical approximation already discussed;\nit is not, by itself, a proof about constituents.\nThe proton and electron produced in neutron decay can bind to form a hydrogen atom. A hydrogen atom is a different system, not a neutron."
    },
    {
      "index": 27,
      "blockIndex": 17,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "A finite lifetime does not, by itself, show that a particle is not elementary.\nThe textbook called the neutron elementary under its historical classification;"
    },
    {
      "index": 28,
      "blockIndex": 17,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "that old description must not be read as a present-day claim that the neutron has no internal structure.\nThe nucleus of heavy hydrogen, **deuterium**, contains a neutron and a proton.\nIt is stable, but stability is not the same as having no constituent parts.\nThe neutron is now known to be a composite particle made of quarks."
    }
  ],
